### The four-case cycle analysis needs CONCRETE cycle arithmetic

Judson's Lemma 10.10 shows any nontrivial normal subgroup of A_n contains a 3-cycle, by
cases on σ's disjoint-cycle shape:

| case | the identity verified |
|---|---|
| σ has a cycle of length > 3 | σ⁻¹(a₁a₂a₃)σ(a₁a₂a₃)⁻¹ = (a₁a₃a_r) |
| σ = τ(a₁a₂a₃)(a₄a₅a₆) | σ⁻¹(a₁a₂a₄)σ(a₁a₂a₄)⁻¹ = (a₁a₄a₂a₆a₃) |
| σ = τ(a₁a₂a₃), τ disjoint 2-cycles | σ² = (a₁a₃a₂) |
| σ = τ(a₁a₂)(a₃a₄) | a further conjugation |

Each is a multiplication of named cycles yielding a named cycle. std has cycle theory
(`std/permutation/cycle.b4m`, `decomposition.b4m`,
`everyPermutationOfAFiniteSetIsAProductOfDisjointCycles`) but no way to COMPUTE such a
product symbolically — the entries a₁…a_r are variables, so this is cycle algebra over
indeterminate points, not evaluation at concrete numbers.

NOT probed as impossible. The honest statement is that it is a substantial piece of new
machinery (a calculus for products of cycles with variable support), and the four identities
above are its acceptance test. Recorded rather than attempted.

**CORRECTED 2026-10-06 — no new machinery is needed.** The "variable support" framing was
wrong. Extensionality reduces each identity to a case analysis on EQUALITY OF POINTS, and
`std/permutation/parity.b4m` already carries the deciders: `transpositionValue` (all three
cases of `(u v)(x)` in one citation), `compositeAtFirstOfInner` / `compositeAtSecondOfInner`
/ `compositeElsewhereOfInner`, and `composedTranspositionsAgree` (extensionality specialized
to a product of two). Judson's four-letter identity `(a,b)(c,d) = (a,c,b)(a,c,d)` is also
STATABLE directly, because std's cycle fold makes a 3-cycle a composite of transpositions:
`cycleSucc` gives `cycle(s,3) = (a₀a₂) ∘ (a₀a₁) ∘ id`, so a 3-cycle needs no new sort.
Verified by proving the value of `(a,b)(c,d)` at `a` strictly (not `--fast`).

The same correction the parity work already recorded for Chapter 5 applies here: these
chapters run over CONCRETE permutations, so the pointwise action lemmas apply. What remains
for §10.2 is PROOF VOLUME (four identities × ~5 cases each, then three lemmas and the
