### LEMMA 10.3's DESIGN — the pivot theorem, and where the real theory starts

Settled 2026-10-07 by probing rather than estimating. TWO of the three things I called
walls were not:

- **the disjoint remainder** — `disjointPermutationsCommute` holds for ARBITRARY
  permutations (not just transpositions, as I wrote), and `supportMember` says support IS
  the moved set. ~60 lines gave `aDisjointFactorFixesWhateverTheOtherMoves`,
  `aCompositeFixesWhatBothFactorsFix` and `aPrefixProductFixesWhatEveryEntryFixes`.
- **variable-length conjugation** — case 2's computation
  `sigma^-1(a1a2a3)sigma(a1a2a3)^-1 = (a1,a3,ar)` needs `sigma^-1` at only THREE points,
  because the conjugated thing is a 3-cycle. No induction over `r`; `conjugateOfACycle`
  (arbitrary length) does it.

  CONFIRMED 2026-10-07 by tracing r = 4, 5, 6, 7 in std's own cycle fold with tau a disjoint
  2-cycle: the identity holds at every r, and `sigma^-1` at the three points is
  `(a_r, a_1, a_2)` — `r` enters ONLY through `sigma^-1(a_1) = a_r`. The missing piece was
  the inverse's action on a cycle's own points, now
  `aCycleInverseRetreats` (`c^-1(a_{i+1}) = a_i` for i < k) and `aCycleInverseWrapsBackward`
  (`c^-1(a_0) = a_k`) in `std/permutation/cycle.b4m` — three short lemmas off
  `cycleAdvances` / `cycleWrapsAround` plus inverse cancellation. That these need only a FEW
  point values is what keeps a conjugation over a variable-length cycle finite.

**THE PIVOT THEOREM — LANDED 2026-10-07** as
`aNonIdentityDecompositionPeelsOffALastCycle` in `std/permutation/decomposition.b4m`:

    decomposesIntoDisjointCycles(f) -> f != identityFn ->
    exists w: Seq; exists k: Nat;
      isACycle(at(w, k)) and subset(support(at(w, k)), support(f))
      and (forall i < k; isACycle(at(w, i)))
      and (forall i < k; subset(support(at(w, i)), support(f)))
      and (forall i, j < k; i != j -> disjointSupports(at(w, i), at(w, j)))
      and f = compose(productUpTo(w, k), at(w, k))
      and disjointSupports(productUpTo(w, k), at(w, k))

**THE SHAPE I RECORDED ABOVE WAS WRONG, and the correction is instructive.** I had it as
`exists t, s, p; f = compose(t, cycle(s, succ(p)))` — peel a NAMED cycle with its own point
list and length. But `decomposesIntoDisjointCycles` produces a `Seq` of cycles plus a run
length, not a `Points`/length pair, so the split is at the SEQUENCE level:
`productUpToSucc` is already an axiom and gives `f = compose(productUpTo(w,q), at(w,q))`
for free. The peeled factor is `at(w, k)`, a cycle by `isACycle`; unpacking it to
`cycle(s, succ(p))` is a separate step a CONSUMER takes when it wants the points.

Consequence: the prefix `productUpTo(w, k)` is itself a shorter product of the same
disjoint cycles — so the recursion cases 3-5 need runs on the RUN LENGTH, with the whole
prefix structure carried along. The named-cycle shape would have thrown that away.

**What was actually missing in std was a CONVERSE.** std had only the directions that
CONSUME disjointness (`disjointSupportsPointwise`,
`aDisjointFactorFixesWhateverTheOtherMoves`). A construction needs to ESTABLISH it: it
knows pointwise that its prefix product and last cycle never move a common point, and owes
the set-level `intersection(support, support) = emptyset`. That is
`disjointSupportsFromPointwise` (`std/permutation.b4m`), plus the sequence-level
`aPrefixProductIsDisjointFromADisjointEntry` (`std/permutation/sequence.b4m`). 763 lines
total. Note the comment already on `aPrefixProductFixesWhatEveryEntryFixes` named this gap
before the tool to close it existed.

MECHANICS worth keeping: `and` is **LEFT**-associative, so a 7-conjunct goal assembles with
`and_intro` from the LEFT and `and_elim_left` on `A and B and C and D` yields `A and B and
C`. And `chain` cannot prove a DISEQUATION — that needs `[by rewrite eq diseq]`.

Why this is the right shape: each of Judson's cases needs only a few NAMED POINTS plus
disjointness of the rest, so the classification is EXISTENTIAL — not a theorem about
multisets of cycle lengths, which is what made it look large. `isACycle(g)` unpacks to
`g = cycle(s, succ(p))` with `distinct(s, succ(p))`, so the length `succ(p)` and the points
`at(s,0)…at(s,p)` are all nameable.

With it, cases 1 and 2 become a split on `p` alone: `p = 2` is a 3-cycle (case 1, done),
`p > 2` is case 2.

**WHERE THE REAL THEORY STARTS.** Cases 3-5 look INSIDE `t` — "t also has a 3-cycle",
"t is all 2-cycles", "t has an even number of further 2-cycles". That is the same
classification one level down, so full Lemma 3 needs a RECURSION on the decomposition
length with the parity invariant (sigma in A_n means an even transposition count) threaded
through. THAT is the part that is design rather than transcription, and it is what remains
after the pivot theorem and cases 1-2.

