### §10.2 PROGRESS — Lemma 1 is PROVED; what Lemma 2 costs

Judson's Lemma 1 ("A_n is generated by 3-cycles") is done, hole-free and strict, in
`std/permutation/alternating.b4m`. Its three identities:

| Judson | status |
|---|---|
| `(a,b)(a,b) = identity` | std's `transpositionIsAnInvolution`, already there |
| `(a,b)(c,d) = (a,c,b)(a,c,d)` | `aPairOfDisjointTranspositionsIsAProductOfThreeCycles` |
| `(a,b)(a,c) = (a,c,b)` | DEFINITIONAL — not a theorem (see below) |

Two findings that make the rest of the section tractable:

**A 3-CYCLE NEEDS NO NEW SORT.** std's cycle fold gives
`cycle(s,3) = (a0 a2) o (a0 a1) o ((a0 a0) o id)` (`cycleSucc` twice, then `cycleZero`),
and `transpositionOfAPointWithItself` kills the degenerate tail. So a 3-cycle IS a
composite of two transpositions sharing their first point — which is why Judson's third
identity is a reading of the definition, and why the second carries all the content.
Proved as `aThreeCycleIsTwoTranspositions`.

**THE METHOD IS EXTENSIONALITY, NOT CYCLE ALGEBRA.** Each identity is: expand both sides
to nested `apply`s (`twoFactorValue` / `fourFactorValue`), evaluate factor by factor with
`transpositionAtFirst`/`AtSecond`/`Elsewhere`, then `funcExtensionality` over a case split
on where the point sits. For identity (2) that is five cases (at a, b, c, d, elsewhere),
each ~60 lines but mechanical once the first is written.

TOOLCHAIN NOTES, all three cost a debugging round:
- `simplify` CANNOT close these joins — it orients each equation left-to-right and the
  middle step must run backward. `chain` is orientation-free and closes them immediately.
- `chain` REJECTS a repeated ref (`chain a b a` is a label-shadowing error); state the
  step once.
- `tautology` sees `a != c` and `c != a` as UNRELATED atoms, so disequality symmetry is a
  real step. std has NO such theorem; `disequalityIsSymmetric` is proved locally in
  `alternating.b4m` and WANTS LIFTING to `std/element.b4m`.

**WHAT LEMMA 2 COSTS.** "If N contains a 3-cycle then N = A_n" needs four more identities
rewriting an arbitrary 3-cycle in terms of the fixed-`(i,j)` family:

    (i,a,j) = (i,j,a)^2
    (i,a,b) = (i,j,b)(i,j,a)^2
    (j,a,b) = (i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)
    (a,b,c) = (i,j,a)^2(i,j,c)(i,j,b)^2(i,j,a)

plus the conjugation `[(i,j)(a,k)](i,j,a)^2[(i,j)(a,k)]^-1 = (i,j,k)`. In transposition
composites the last identity is EIGHT factors, so it needs a `sixFactorValue` /
`eightFactorValue` in the same style, and each identity splits on more points (up to five
distinct letters). Same method, more volume — no new machinery, and no new primitive.
Estimate from Lemma 1's shape: ~6 helper theorems and ~5 point-cases per identity.

### SIMPLICITY is stated per-subgroup, because there is no sort of subgroups

`isSimpleWitness` in `std/group/normal.b4m` is
`isTrivialSubgroup or isTheWholeGroup` — the property of THE subgroup that file carries, not
"for all normal subgroups N of G". Quantifying over subgroups needs a sort of subgroups, the
same limit Chapter 9 hit for ≅ (CHAPTER9-PAINPOINTS: "2b4m can state a theory and MODEL one
theory in another, but cannot talk about theories as objects").

The workable shape is the one std already uses for subgroups generally: a consumer declares
its own membership predicate and proves the disjunction for it. `std/subgroup.b4m` has a
second predicate `inSubgroup2` for exactly this reason (two subgroups at once), which is the
declare-what-you-need pattern rather than a limitation.

## WHAT WORKED, worth keeping

### A coset is its MEMBERSHIP PREDICATE, and Judson's equalities survive that

There is no sort of subsets of a group, so §10.1's `gN = Ng` is extensional agreement of two
predicates and `aN = bN` is `inSubgroup(a⁻¹b)` (§6.1's
`cosetsCoincideIffQuotientIsInTheSubgroup`). Both are exactly how Judson USES them — he never
needs the coset as an object, only membership and equality — so the transliteration is
faithful rather than a workaround. `coset.b4m` develops only the left coset (Lagrange needs
one, and the proofs mirror), so `inRightCoset` is defined in `normal.b4m`.

### `--axioms` does not see inside a `simplify` rewrite set

Dropping aliases that `--axioms` reported unused removed `invProduct`, which IS cited — by
the `simplify` step in `theFactorOperationIsWellDefined`. The check caught it immediately
("reference not found"), but worth knowing: `--axioms` attributes what a proof RESTS on, and
a rewrite named in a `simplify` set does not show up there. Same happened with
`subgroupHasIdentity` when §10.2's work later needed it.

### STAGE A LONG REWRITE CHAIN — nine equations exceeded `chain`'s search

The conjugation step needed nine equations to get from `sigma(i,j,a)^2 sigma^-1` to
`(i,j,k)`. One `chain` with all nine failed ("cannot connect … from the cited equations"),
and so did one `simplify` (two of the links must run backward). What worked: SPLIT at the
orientation boundary —

    @conjugate-is-the-square | … = (j,i,k)^2   [using simplify <the seven left-to-right links>]
    @conclusion-conjugate    | … = (i,j,k)     [using chain conjugate-is-the-square <the two reversible ones>]

`simplify` for the stretch that is all left-to-right, `chain` for the short tail that
needs reorientation. Neither tactic has to span the whole distance.

### `specialize` NEEDS TERM ARGS FOR BINDERS THAT FOLLOW A PREMISE

`conjugationIsMultiplicative` is `forall sigma; invertible(sigma) -> forall f, g; …`.
Writing `specialize conjugationIsMultiplicative(sigma) sigma-is-a-permutation` leaves `f`
and `g` UNBOUND, and the diagnostic shows the remainder as a `forall b1: Fn; forall b2: Fn;
…` — accurate but easy to misread as a shape mismatch. The fix is the rule
CHAPTER6-PAINPOINTS already records for `fullOrderGenerates`: put EVERY term in the parens
regardless of where its binder sits, `specialize thm(sigma, f, g) <premises>`.

### A STATEMENT'S HYPOTHESIS MAY BE UNUSED BY ITS PROOF, AND THAT IS AN ERROR

The conjugation statement carried `a != k` because Judson's `sigma = (i,j)(a,k)` is a
product of two genuine transpositions. The proof never needs it —
`transpositionIsAPermutation` holds for a degenerate transposition too — so the
unused-fact check rejected it and the hypothesis had to come out. Worth knowing when
transcribing: the book states distinctness for mathematical context, and the formal proof
only takes what it cites.

### REACH FOR `assoc(lemma)` WHEN THE WORK IS REGROUPING — not hand-written assoc steps

Corollary of the entry below, and of the N-helper treadmill above. Proving
`sigma o sigma = id` for `sigma = (i,j)(a,k)` needs the product regrouped so the middle
pair is adjacent, and I wrote FOUR hand instantiations of `composeAssoc` to do it —
`composeAssoc(h, g, f)` gives `(h∘g)∘f = h∘(g∘f)`, so each one has to be aimed by hand and
the error when you miss is a whole-term expected-vs-got.

`[using assoc(composeAssoc)]` right-nests BOTH sides and compares, so each regrouping is
one step with nothing to aim. Every one of the four collapsed.

The rule: `simplify` rewrites to a normal form and will not reassociate to expose a
cancellation (below); `assoc(lemma)` exists precisely for the reassociation. If the step is
"the same factors, differently grouped", that is the tactic.

### `simplify` will not reassociate to expose a cancellation

`op(c, op(inverse(c), d)) = d` fails as a `simplify` goal even with `grpAssoc`,
`grpInverseRight` and `grpIdentityLeft` in the set — it will not reassociate the nest to put
`c` next to `inverse(c)`. Four explicit steps (regroup, cancel, identity, chain) do it, after
which `simplify` closes the surrounding algebra happily. Consistent with the cheat-sheet's
framing of `simplify` as "rewriting to a shared normal form" rather than a search.

**CASES 3 AND 5 ARE NOW PROVED (2026-10-13) — AND THE CLASSIFICATION GAP IS GONE ENTIRELY.**

Probing before writing, a fourth time, retired the last of the three "missing machinery"
claims. The five-case list is real as Judson's exposition, but it is NOT a case analysis the
formalization has to perform: all five cases are instances of three length-free, structure-free
theorems, so **no cycle-type classification theorem is needed at all.**

Case 5 (`sigma = tau (a1a2)(a3a4)`). Judson conjugates by `(a1a2a3)`, reaches `(a1a3)(a2a4)`,
and conjugates AGAIN. The probe shows why the first conjugation does not finish: the commutator
is another double transposition (`{a1->a3, a2->a4}`), support 4, both with `tau` trivial and
with `tau = (a5a6)(a7 z)` attached — so the second conjugation is genuinely required, not an
artifact of Judson's bookkeeping. What the probe then found is that the second conjugation needs
NO 2-cycle structure:

    sigma i = j, sigma j = i, sigma a = a   ==>   sigma (i,j,a) sigma^-1 (i,j,a)^-1 = (i,j,a)

Only sigma's action AT THE THREE POINTS matters — it swaps two of them and fixes the third.
That is exactly `(a1a3)(a2a4)` against the 3-cycle `(a1,a3,a5)`, and `a5` is where `n >= 5` is
spent. Three theorems:

- `theCommutatorWithASwapOfTwoOfItsPoints` — the value, `T(j,a) T(i,a)`, by specializing
  case 1's `theCommutatorOfAThreeCycleWithAPermutation` at the three images and cancelling the
  middle `T(j,i) T(i,j)` with symmetry plus the involution law.
- `aSwappedCommutatorIsTheThreeCycle` — that value IS `(i,j,a)`. It is not in std's
  shared-first-point fold `(x,y,z) = T(x,z) T(x,y)`; flipping both factors onto `a` with
  `transpositionIsSymmetric` gives `T(a,j) T(a,i)`, and `aThreeCycleIsInvariantUnderRotation`
  at `(a,i,j)` is literally that equation. Distinctness is needed and only partly — the
  identity fails at `i = j` and at `j = a`, and `i = a` is never consulted; both are free to a
  caller whose points are a 3-cycle's.
- `theCommutatorWithASwapIsAThreeCycle` — the two composed, which is case 5 as a caller wants
  it: `sigma` in `N` gives the commutator in `N`, and the commutator is a 3-cycle.

Case 3 (`sigma = tau (a1a2a3)(a4a5a6)`) needs no new theorem. Conjugating by `(a1a2a4)` and
multiplying gives a 5-CYCLE (`(a1 a4 a3 a5 a2)`, support 5, confirmed), exactly as Judson says
— and case 1's engine then applies to THAT, because it reads only three points and never the
length. Chained end to end in the probe: the commutator of the 5-cycle with the 3-cycle on its
first three orbit points has support 3. So case 3 reduces to case 1 mechanically, with no
intermediate classification.

**WHY THE CLASSIFICATION GAP WAS ILLUSORY, stated once.** The list above frames the cases by
CYCLE TYPE, which is a property of the whole permutation and would need the classification
theorem to establish. Every case's actual proof reads sigma at THREE POINTS. Cycle type is how
Judson FINDS the three points to use; it is not a hypothesis any of the algebra consumes. So the
formalization's hypotheses are point-values (`sigma i = j`, `sigma a = a`) and distinctness, all
of which a caller discharges from its decomposition — and the "real induction over the multiset
of cycle lengths plus the parity constraint" I scoped at the top of this file is work that does
not exist.

Score on this file's predictions: FOUR overstated gaps, zero that survived a probe. The method
is unchanged and is the only reason they were caught — verify computationally FIRST, then look
for the statement that drops the structure.

**LEMMA 10.9'S TRANSITIVITY IS NOW PROVED (2026-10-13), and it needed none of the machinery
this file predicted.** That makes FIVE overstated gaps, zero survivors.

The claim carried above, repeatedly, was that transitivity needs "any triple of distinct points
maps to any other by some sigma IN A_n", with "n >= 5 buys the two spare points that fix sigma's
parity". That framing is what made it look like it required BUILDING a permutation from a
prescribed finite map — machinery std does not have and which would be a substantial
contribution. Two probes collapsed it.

FIRST, conjugation acts on 3-CYCLES, not on triples. A 3-cycle equals each of its three
rotations (`aThreeCycleIsInvariantUnderRotation`), so a target need only be matched up to
rotation. Measured: a conjugator that is a product of two transpositions reaches ALL 20
3-cycles on five points from any one of them, while carrying only 2160 of 3600 TRIPLE pairs.
The triple framing was strictly harder than the problem.

SECOND, and this is the whole lemma, there is a UNIFORM conjugator with no parity repair at all.
Searching the solver's choices symbolically rather than guessing formulas (four guesses scored
0/120 each) turned up `T(a,c) T(a,b)` — which IS the 3-cycle `(a,b,c)` in std's fold:

    (a,b,c) (i,j,a) (a,b,c)^-1 = (i,j,b)

Exact on all 120/720/2520 configurations at n = 5, 6, 7. Being a 3-cycle, the conjugator is a
product of exactly two transpositions, hence EVEN BY CONSTRUCTION and already in A_n — so the
"two spare points to fix sigma's parity" step never arises. `b` and `c` are simply the two
points outside {i,j,a}, which is the honest place n >= 5 is spent: a point to move TO and a
point to park the displaced one on.

`conjugatingAThreeCycleMovesItsThirdPoint` is that identity. A caller chains it: under the move
the 3-cycles on five points have DIAMETER 3 (BFS from every one of the 20), one application per
point, so reaching an arbitrary 3-cycle is three uses composed — not an induction.

TWO PROCESS NOTES, both worth more than the lemma.

- **`a != c` is NOT a hypothesis.** My draft stated it; the proof never consulted it, and the
  identity holds at a = c too (the conjugator degenerates to `T(a,b)`, which still carries a to
  b — checked over all 1080 six-point configurations with a = c allowed). Dropping it left every
  step intact. An unused hypothesis in a signature is the invented-hypothesis failure mode in
  miniature: it reads as a condition the mathematics requires when it is only a condition the
  author assumed.
- **`--fast` passed what strict REJECTED**, the fast/strict invariant violation. Three
  evaluation steps were written `[using simplify ... permutation.transpositionElsewhere ...]`,
  and strict answered `'permutation.transpositionElsewhere' is not an equation` — it is
  conditional (`x != u -> x != v -> ...`), so it cannot be a rewrite. The fix is the file's own
  established idiom: `specialize transpositionElsewhere(u, v, x)` with the two disequalities to
  get an equation step, THEN simplify on that. Iterating only on `--fast` would have hidden this
  until the gate.

**THE TWIST, AND WHERE n >= 5 IS ACTUALLY SPENT (2026-10-13).** A correction to my own entry
above, which claimed the one-point move was all of transitivity.

Chaining the one-point move is NOT a formula. I sketched a three-move chain (move the third
point, rotate, repeat) and checked it: 2520 of 3600 3-cycle pairs FAIL it. The earlier BFS
result (diameter 3) is still right, but it measures reachability as PERMUTATIONS, choosing a
fresh rotation representative at each step; my chain fixed the representatives and so was
strictly weaker. Reading the BFS's own shortest paths shows why no formula captures it — the
representatives it picks vary per target.

What the BFS distance histogram DOES hand over is the structure: {0:1, 1:6, 2:12, 3:1}. Exactly
ONE 3-cycle sits at distance 3 from any given one, and it is that 3-cycle's TWIST. Everything
else is two moves or fewer. So the twist is the orbit's far point and deserves its own direct
conjugator rather than a chain.

It has one, uniform and exact (120/720/2520 configurations at n = 5, 6, 7):

    (T(i,j) T(b,c)) (i,j,a) (T(i,j) T(b,c))^-1 = (i,a,j)

`conjugatingAThreeCycleByASwapAndASpareSwapGivesItsTwist`. The conjugator does two unrelated
jobs and separating them is the whole insight: `T(i,j)` does the mathematics — it swaps two of
the 3-cycle's points and fixes the third, and the relabelling of `(i,j,a)` under that IS the
twist — while `T(b,c)` touches the 3-cycle not at all (`b`, `c` lie outside `{i,j,a}`) and
exists ONLY to make the conjugator even, so it lies in A_n. A single transposition would
relabel identically and be odd.

**So "n >= 5 buys two spare points to fix sigma's parity" is finally located, and it is much
narrower than this file claimed for weeks.** It is not a fact about transitivity in general —
the one-point move needs no parity repair at all, its conjugator being a 3-cycle and hence even
by construction. The twist is the lone case that needs it, because the only relabelling
producing a twist is a single swap. Every previous statement of that phrase here attached it to
the wrong thing.

Proof route: `theConjugateOfAThreeCycleRelabelsItsPoints` at the conjugator (images i -> j,
j -> i, a -> a), then the relabelled `T(j,a) T(j,i)` is the twist by TWO instances of
`aThreeCycleIsInvariantUnderRotation` composed — at (a,j,i) and at (i,a,j). One instance does
not suffice; the factors are swapped on both sides, which the exhaustive instance search showed
before any proof was written.

**POINT SUPPLY IS NOW IN std (2026-10-13), and it located a real gap rather than an imagined
one.** The entry above said the twist conjugator needs two points outside {i,j,a}; both it and
the one-point move take those points as HYPOTHESES, so something has to produce them.

Nothing could, and the reason is worth writing down: **`Element` is an unconstrained sort.**
`std/element.b4m` declares it with no operations and no axioms, deliberately. So "there are at
least five points" is not a fact about `Element` that one could prove, nor one that could be
refuted — it simply has no expression there. Every earlier statement of the n >= 5 hypothesis in
this file was therefore informal in a way I had not noticed: the theorems said "given these five
distinct points", never "five exist".

std already had the convention for this and it was not a new mechanism: bound the domain with a
SET carrying `hasSize`, the way `decomposition.finiteSupportDecomposes` bounds a support. Two
theorems in `std/set/finite.b4m`:

- `aDistinctPointSurvivesARemoval` — a point survives the removal of a different one, factored
  out so the four peels below are uniform.
- `aSetOfAtLeastFiveHasAFifthPoint` — a set of size 5+m has a member outside any four named
  ones. `removingAnElementDropsTheSize` four times, then `sizedSetHasAMember` on the remainder
  (size succ(m), so nonempty); the four disequalities come back out of the nested differences.

THE BOUND IS FIVE, NOT SIX, and the reason is the two consumers: the one-point move needs a
point to move TO and a point to park the displaced one on, both outside the three in hand; the
twist needs two points outside the three whose swap makes the conjugator even. Four named plus
one supplied is the worst case. That is the n >= 5 of Judson's hypothesis, finally stated as
something the kernel checks.

TWO CHOICES WORTH RECORDING.

- **Iterated removal beat the slicker route.** `properSubsetIsSmaller` gives a contradiction
  argument — if every member were one of the four, u would be a subset of a 4-set — but it needs
  the four-point set BUILT and SIZED (three insertions carrying six non-membership side
  conditions) plus a subset proof. Iterated removal needs no set construction at all. Counted
  both before writing either.
- **The disequality hypotheses are oriented for the peels** (`q != p`, not `p != q`) because
  `finite.b4m` cannot reach a symmetry lemma — `disequalityIsSymmetric` lives in
  `permutation/alternating.b4m`, ABOVE set theory, and importing it would be a cyclical
  dependency. Callers flip with their own lemma. Writing the statement in the "natural" order and
  then needing symmetry inside set theory would have been the wrong fix.

**LEMMA 10.9'S SELECTOR IS BUILT (2026-10-14), and the leaf count went 13 -> 5 -> 7.**

The selector decides which of Judson's identities an arbitrary 3-cycle needs. Three successive
scopings of it, each corrected by measurement:

1. **13 leaves, no collapse.** Split on which coordinate of (x,y,z) is i and which is j: 4x4
   minus the 3 same-coordinate clashes. Canonical rotation is unique per leaf (verified over all
   120 triples on six points), so positions do not merge.
2. **"5 leaves, the rest need nothing."** `rotationCarriesMembership` (a rotation rewrite
   carries membership) made each leaf cheap, and I classified 3 as rotation-only and 3 as
   zero-rotation restatements of their shape theorem — leaving 5 to prove. Those 5 were
   generated against a validated template and all went strict-green.
3. **Wrong: it was 7.** Mapping every leaf GOAL to a named proved theorem — mechanically, not by
   reading — found two gaps, `(p,i,j)` and `(j,p,i)`. The family premise supplies `(i,j,p)`;
   those goals are its ROTATIONS, and a rotation still wants a theorem. The classification was
   right that they need no shape theorem and no product closure, wrong that they need nothing.

So the honest count is 13 leaves: 1 closed by the family premise directly, 12 by named theorems.
The full table maps each (which-coordinate-is-i, which-is-j) case to its theorem with the
explicit variable substitution, checked by script rather than by eye — which is what caught the
two gaps, and what confirmed all 13 map cleanly once they were closed.

**PATTERNS PROBED BEFORE USE, each in a throwaway file.** Every one of these would have been a
confusing failure inside a 500-line generated proof:

- **Nested schema instantiation.** A selector leaf is a schema over `inN` citing a shape theorem
  that is also a schema over `inN`, so it instantiates the inner one AT ITS OWN PARAMETER:
  `[using instantiation innerSchema((fun g: Fn => inQ(g))) refs]`. Works.
- **`case` arm restatement.** Inside a `case` arm, the arm label names a SUBPROOF, so
  `[using tautology <arm-label>]` is an error ("names a subproof; a step reference is required").
  The arm's assumption must first be restated with `[by hypothesis <arm-label>]`.
- **The `isAThreeCycle` unpack.** It is a `define` expanding to a nested existential, so the
  bridge from "every distinct triple" to "every 3-cycle" is three unpack levels with the
  conjunction projected by `tautology`. Promoted to
  `everyTripleInTheSubgroupMeansEveryThreeCycle` rather than inlined, since every consumer needs
  exactly that shape.

**AND A TRAP WORTH NAMING: an uninstantiated schema reports "0 theorems proven" and nothing
else.** Not an error, not a warning. I lost a cycle to this twice — once on a typo'd step label
in the capstone that checked green until the instantiation existed, once on a probe I thought had
validated a leaf when it had checked nothing at all. A schema with no instantiation is unverified
and SILENT about it, which is the strongest argument for `--library`'s rule that every schema be
exercised where it is declared.

**THE FLAT DISJUNCTION DOES NOT WORK, and the countermodel says exactly why (2026-10-14).**

I tried to replace the nested 4x4 position split with one flat 13-way disjunction — simpler to
generate, simpler to read. `tautology` refused it:

    countermodel: x = i := true, y = j := false, z = j := false,
                  x = j := true, y = i := false, z = i := false

It set `x = i` AND `x = j` both true. With `i != j` in hand that is impossible, but `tautology`
sees `x = i`, `x = j` and `i != j` as three unrelated opaque atoms and cannot link them. Adding
`i != j` as a premise does not help, for exactly the same reason. This is the documented
limitation, and the countermodel named the missing link precisely.

The nested split works because each LEVEL is a genuine propositional tautology over its own
atoms: level 1 is 4-way over `x=i`, `y=i`, `z=i`; level 2, inside each arm, is 4-way over the
`j` atoms. Nothing cross-links.

And the three same-coordinate clashes never need ruling out propositionally. In the arm where
`x = i` and `x = j`, the leaf derives `i = j` by symmetry-and-rewrite and discharges against
`i != j` with `ex_falso` — a kernel step, which is the standard remedy for this exact failure
("do the non-propositional step yourself, then let tautology close it"). So the tree is 4x4 = 16
arms: the 13 real leaves plus 3 contradiction arms, and the extra three are the cheapest in the
proof.

Probed in a throwaway file before generating anything, like the other four patterns.

**THE ASSEMBLY PLAN, VERIFIED MECHANICALLY BEFORE GENERATION (2026-10-14).**

Four things checked by script rather than by reading, each of which would have surfaced as a
confusing failure partway through a 500-line generated proof:

1. **Every leaf's GOAL matches its schema's CONCLUSION** under an explicit variable
   substitution. 13/13. This is the check that found the two missing rotation leaves.
2. **Every schema's stated conclusion matches what the file actually proves** — parsed out of
   `alternating-identities.b4m` rather than trusted from my spec. 12/12.
3. **Every leaf's ARGUMENTS are fully pinned.** Binders not appearing in a conclusion came out
   "UNPINNED" on the first pass; they are the fixed points `i`/`j`, which the assembly has in
   scope, so filling them is mechanical — but silently passing the wrong one would have been a
   long debug.
4. **Every leaf's required DISEQUALITIES are derivable in its arm.** This one needed two passes.
   The first reported 12 of 13 leaves missing facts like `z != i` in the arm where `x = i`; that
   was my checker substituting in one direction only. Substituting both ways — `z != x` with
   `x = i` gives `z != i` — clears all 13.

Worth noting that (4)'s first result was a false alarm from the CHECKER, not the plan, and the
fix was to make the checker as capable as a `rewrite` step is. A plan-verification script that
is weaker than the proof system will report gaps that are not there; one that is stronger will
miss gaps that are. Matching it to the available inference is the whole trick.

**`simplify` CANNOT PROVE A DISEQUATION (2026-10-14).** Deriving `z != i` from `z != x` and
`x = i` looks like equational rewriting, and `[using simplify x-is-i z-not-x]` is the natural
spelling. It fails: `simplify: goal is not an equation`. The accelerant rewrites to a shared
normal form and needs an EQUATION goal; a disequation is not one.

`[by rewrite x-is-i z-not-x]` does it — the kernel rule substitutes into any formula, including
a disequation, and is bidirectional. Every one of the assembly's 16 arms needs two of these
(the free coordinates' disequalities against i and j), so getting it wrong once would have been
getting it wrong 32 times.

Caught by probing arm (0,1) complete — statement, derivations, conclusion, AND an instantiation
— before generating the other fifteen.

**THE ASSEMBLY'S DISEQUALITY PLAN IS NOW CONCRETE (2026-10-14): 54 steps, all mechanical.**

Two attempts at a recursive disequality solver inside the emitter accumulated edge cases
(flip-vs-rewrite, substituting a coordinate for a fixed point vs the reverse, multi-substitution
chains) and were the wrong shape. Replaced with a PRECOMPUTED TABLE: per arm, the exact ordered
list of disequality steps with each one's justification, derived once by script.

The result is 54 steps across the 13 real arms, every one of exactly two forms:

    flip     a != b   from   b != a       [using specialize alternating.disequalityIsSymmetric]
    rewrite  a != b   from   c != b       [by rewrite <position-equality> <that fact>]

Per-arm counts run 2 to 6, totalling 54. No arm needs a chain of two substitutions, which is
what the recursive version was built to handle and never had to.

The lesson is the generator-design one: when the emitter grows branches for cases that may not
arise, compute the answer for every case first and look at the table. Here the table showed the
hard case was absent.

**ALL 13 LEAF BODIES GENERATE AND VERIFY STRICTLY (2026-10-14).** Each wrapped as a standalone
schema with a concrete instantiation at A_n, so each gets a real verdict rather than the silent
"0 theorems proven" of an uninstantiated schema.

The generator took four iterations, and every one was the SAME defect in a different place:
**emitting a fact nothing cites.** `2b4m` rejects that outright ("unused fact: step 'X' is never
used"), which is the right call and made each round a one-line diagnosis, but the shape is worth
recording because a generator invites it:

1. Pre-ensuring BOTH orientations of every disequality — `i != x` and `x != i` — when each leaf
   consumes exactly one. Fixed by emitting lazily, at the point of use.
2. Restating all four outer hypotheses in every leaf, when the smaller leaves cite two or three.
   Fixed by generating the body first and emitting only the restatements its text references.
3. Projecting every conjunct of the arm's bundled assumption, when a leaf uses a subset. Fixed
   with an on-demand `project()` hook — which then had to be threaded INTO the disequality
   planner, since `ensure()` is what discovers which conjuncts are wanted.
4. A strip-regex matching `given-closure]` when testing for `closure]`, so the closure
   restatement survived in the three leaves that need no product closure. Fixed with a
   negative-lookbehind boundary.

The pattern: a hand-written proof cites what it needs because the author writes the citation
first. A generator emits a supply and hopes it matches the demand. Making the emitter DEMAND-DRIVEN
— body first, facts second, each on request — removed all four at once, and is the shape to reach
for directly next time.

**THE LEAF BODIES NOW TAKE THEIR POSITION FACTS FROM TWO SEPARATE ARMS (2026-10-14).**

The first working generator had each leaf restate ONE bundled assumption (`x = i and y = j`) and
project its conjuncts by `tautology`. That shape cannot survive the nested tree, where the two
facts are assumed by two different `case` arms — the outer one for i, the inner for j.

The fix made the emitter simpler, not more complex: a position fact assumed by its own arm is
ONE `[by hypothesis when-<arm>]` step, with no projection at all. Only the `no-i` / `no-j` arms
still project, since those assume a three-way conjunction (`x != i and y != i and z != i`), and
that restatement is emitted once per arm on first use.

So the final leaf shape is: one or three hypothesis restatements per arm, the arm's derived
disequalities (flip or single rewrite), the cited theorem instantiated at `inN`, and one rewrite
per position equality to turn the canonical form back into `(x,y,z)`. All 13 verify strictly in
this form.

**I RE-DERIVED LEMMA 10.10 INSTEAD OF READING IT, AND GOT A WORSE PROOF (2026-10-14).**

Asked to finish 10.11, I probed for a uniform conjugator rule, found that "(p,q,r) at a moved
point" covers most sigma but that the exceptional set GROWS with n ((3,3) at n=6, plus (3,2,2)
at n=7, plus (2,2,2,2) at n=8), and concluded Lemma 10.10 needs an INDUCTION ON SUPPORT SIZE
with a search for the conjugator at each step. I sized that as "larger than the selector, and
without the selector's advantage", and said so.

That is not Judson's proof, and Judson's is better. His four cases, each verified exactly as
stated (probe over the relevant points):

    sigma has a cycle of length r > 3    sigma^-1 (a1a2a3) sigma (a1a2a3)^-1 = (a1 a3 ar)
    sigma = tau (a1a2a3)(a4a5a6)         sigma^-1 (a1a2a4) sigma (a1a2a4)^-1 = (a1 a4 a2 a6 a3)
    sigma = tau (a1a2a3), tau involutive sigma^2 = (a1 a3 a2)
    sigma = tau (a1a2)(a3a4)             two conjugations, support 4 then 3

The second lands a 5-CYCLE, which is the FIRST case's shape — so the cases reduce to each
other and the analysis TERMINATES. There is no descent, no induction on support, and no search:
each conjugator is READ OFF the cycle shape, which is exactly what the case split gives you.

What went wrong is the same error this file already records twice, in a new direction. Both
earlier times I claimed machinery was missing and a probe disproved it. This time I claimed
machinery was NEEDED — a support induction — because I probed for a uniform rule, found none,
and generalized from that to "therefore induction" without checking what the source does. The
cases ARE the structure that makes a uniform rule unnecessary; searching for one and failing
says nothing about whether the textbook proof needs it.

THE RULE, stated for next time: when the task is transcribing a known proof, read the proof's
OWN case structure before probing for a better one. A probe tells you whether YOUR idea works;
it cannot tell you the author had a different idea. (`agents/2b4m-cheatsheet.md` already says
bottom-up when the statement is GIVEN — this is the same lesson one level up: the PROOF STRATEGY
is given too, not just the statement.)

