THREE THINGS WORTH KEEPING.

- **The hypothesis set a pointwise proof needs is LARGER than the identity needs.** Case 2's
  identity holds assuming only `a1 != a6` and `a3 != a6` (verified over 2000 assignments), and I
  tried to state it that weakly. The pointwise proofs need all ten disequalities, because each
  trace must know which branch of `transposition` applies at every factor. The generator said so
  at once ("no source for a4 != a1"). Costs nothing here — Judson's two 3-cycles are disjoint —
  but the general lesson is that minimal hypotheses for a TRUTH and for a PROOF differ.

- **Case 4 step 1 needed no extensionality and no hypotheses at all.** It is a cancellation:
  the middle pair `T(a2,a1) T(a1,a2)` is the identity by symmetry plus the involution law. The
  other three cases each needed a five- or six-case pointwise split; this one is four algebra
  steps. Checking whether a case is a cancellation before reaching for extensionality is worth
  the ten seconds.

- **The factor-order convention bit once.** std's cycle fold makes `(n0 n1 ... nk)` equal
  `T(n0,nk) o ... o T(n0,n1)`, outermost first. My trace script listed factors in the other
  order and reported the case-2 identity FALSE at every point. The identity was fine; the script
  was wrong. A trace that fails at every point is far more likely to be a convention error than
  a false identity.

**THE CASE DISPATCH NEEDS NO ORBIT THEORY (2026-10-14).** I expected this to be where the
cycle-type classification genuinely lives — "a statement about sigma's orbit structure", as I
wrote a moment before measuring it. It is not.

Each identity consumes POINT-VALUES of sigma^-1, so the dispatch only has to produce a labelling
of points for which one of four value-patterns holds:

    case 1   si a1 = ar, si a2 = a1, si a3 = a2          (four distinct points)
    case 4   si a1 = a2, si a2 = a1, si a3 = a4          (four distinct points)
    case 2   si a1 = a3, si a2 = a1, si a4 = a6          (five distinct points)
    case 3   sigma IS a 3-cycle                           (nothing to prove)

Checked exhaustively over every non-identity even permutation at n = 5, 6, 7 (59, 359, 2519 of
them): the first three patterns cover everything EXCEPT cycle type (3,), and that exception is
precisely case 3, where N already contains a 3-cycle. Zero genuinely uncovered.

So the dispatch is an existential over point labellings, not a classification of sigma. The
point-value framing that made each individual case length-free does the same work one level up:
it converts "which cycle type is sigma" into "which pattern do some of sigma's point-values
match", and the latter needs no decomposition walk at all.

That is now FIVE times in this chapter that a structural-classification requirement dissolved
once the statement was written over point-values. Worth promoting from observation to method:
when a case analysis looks like it needs cycle type, first ask what the cases' proofs actually
read off sigma, and check whether THAT is coverable.

**THE DISPATCH'S GROUP-THEORY STEP, and a direction error I nearly shipped (2026-10-14).**

Judson's case computations are written `sigma^-1 c sigma c^-1`, and it is tempting to read that
as "conjugate c by sigma^-1, times c inverse". That reading is useless: c is a 3-cycle, not an
element of N, so conjugating it by something gives nothing about N.

The correct reading groups it the other way:

    sigma^-1 c sigma c^-1  =  sigma^-1 * (c sigma c^-1)

- `c sigma c^-1` is in N by NORMALITY (sigma in N, c in A_n),
- `sigma^-1` is in N by closure under inverses,
- so the product is in N by closure under products — and Judson's identity says that product
  is a 3-cycle.

My first dispatch arm applied normality correctly and then rewrote the result against an
identity about a DIFFERENT expression (the conjugate of the 3-cycle rather than the commutator).
It would have failed at the kernel, but it is the kind of mismatch that reads as correct on the
page, and the earlier mis-trace recorded in this file was the same class of error: getting the
conjugation ORDER right and the GROUPING wrong.

Checked computationally before rewriting: `sigma^-1 c sigma c^-1` and `sigma^-1 * (c sigma c^-1)`
agree, so the regrouping is sound and the normality route is the one to build.

**THE --fast ALIAS BUG: REPRODUCED, ROOT-CAUSED, FIXED (2026-10-14).**

SYMPTOM: `[using specialize <alias>]` passed strict and failed `--fast` with "unknown
reference" — the fast/strict invariant violated in the PERMISSIVE direction, which is the
nonsensical one: `--fast` admits accelerated verdicts instead of demanding certificates, so
anything strict accepts it must accept.

ROOT CAUSE, in `Prove.namesAFact` (`src/Engine/ProveTask/Prove.zig`). An admitted accelerant
builds no certificate, so the admit path walks its refs only to seed use-all-facts roots, and
`seedAndCheckLocalRoot` diagnoses a ref naming neither a local step nor a global fact as a
typo. That guard is deliberate and correct — it exists so `--fast` cannot accept a typo'd ref
strict would reject. But `namesAFact` decided "global fact" by switching over the file's own
AST declaration:

    .axiom, .hole, .theorem => true,
    else => false,

written before the `fact` keyword existed. An alias is `ast.Decl.fact` (ast.zig:233), a
variant distinct from `.theorem`, so it fell through and every aliased lemma looked like a typo.

THE TELLING DETAIL: the function's own doc comment says it "counts an ALIAS to a fact too" and
explains exactly why that matters. The comment was right; the code had stopped matching it.
Reading the comment would have confirmed the intended behaviour and hidden the bug — only the
switch body shows it.

FIX: add `.fact` to the switch. One token. Verified the typo guard still fires in both modes
afterwards, so the fix is narrow rather than a loosening.

WHY IT HID: it needs an ACCELERANT citing an alias by bare local name under `--fast`. The
corpus habit of qualifying inline avoids it; `by cite <alias>` is unaffected (different
resolution path, which does count aliases). It surfaced only once
`alternating-identities.b4m` accumulated enough aliased citations to produce 20+ bogus errors
at once — and cost a cycle believing I had broken a green file.

NARROWING worth keeping: my original repro header claimed the bug needed two theorems in the
file. It does not; one suffices. And a LOCAL theorem cited identically works fine, which is
what isolated the alias as the trigger rather than `specialize` itself.

**ALL FOUR DISPATCH ARMS ARE PROVED (2026-10-14).** Each takes a point-value pattern plus N's
closure properties and puts a cycle in N:

    arm 1  long cycle        aLongCyclePatternPutsAThreeCycleInTheSubgroup        -> 3-cycle
    arm 2  two 3-cycles      twoDisjointThreeCyclesPutAFiveCycleInTheSubgroup     -> 5-CYCLE
    arm 3  invol. x 3-cycle  anInvolutionTimesAThreeCyclePutsAThreeCycleInTheSubgroup -> 3-cycle
    arm 4  disjoint 2-cycles disjointTwoCyclesPutAThreeCycleInTheSubgroup         -> 3-cycle

THREE THINGS THE ARMS TAUGHT, none of which was visible from the identities alone.

- **Arm 2's conclusion is a 5-cycle, not a 3-cycle.** That is what makes Judson's analysis
  TERMINATE rather than recurse: the caller feeds the 5-cycle back to arm 1. Verified which
  pattern arm 1 then matches — the 5-cycle's inverse sends a1 -> a3, a4 -> a1, a2 -> a4, so arm
  1 applies at (a1, a4, a2, a3), walking the 5-cycle backwards, and yields (a1, a2, a3). All
  four points distinct.

- **Arm 3 needs NO normality.** sigma^2 is a product of two elements of N, so product closure
  alone suffices. The other three arms each need a conjugate. That asymmetry is Judson's — his
  case 3 squares rather than conjugating — and noticing it saved writing the normality
  machinery into an arm that cannot use it.

- **Every identity had to be REGROUPED, and the grouping differs per case.** Judson writes
  commutators; N's closure sees products of specific shapes. Case 1 regroups to
  `sigma^-1 * (c sigma c^-1)` — the conjugated element is sigma. Case 4 step 2 regroups to
  `sigma * (c sigma^-1 c^-1)` — the conjugated element is sigma^INVERSE, because the
  commutator's factor order differs. Each was verified computationally before writing, which is
  the only reason the second one did not land as a late kernel failure.

Where n >= 5 is spent is now ONE named place: arm 4's step 2 needs a point the double
transposition fixes, i.e. outside {a1,a2,a3,a4}. Its four disequalities are the arm's only
extra hypotheses, dischargeable from `set_finite.aSetOfAtLeastFiveHasAFifthPoint`.

WHAT REMAINS for unconditional Theorem 10.11 is one theorem: that every non-identity even
permutation MATCHES one of the four patterns. The coverage is measured (exhaustive at
n = 5, 6, 7); the proof is not written, and needs two peels of
`aNonIdentityDecompositionPeelsOffALastCycle`, two length splits, and one parity argument
(an even permutation is not a lone transposition). All four inputs exist in std.

**THE COVERING PROOF'S LENGTH->=4 BRANCH IS PROVED (2026-10-14).**
`cycle.aLongCycleMatchesTheFirstPattern`: from `distinct(s, succ^4(q))` alone, all NINE of
dispatch arm 1's hypotheses — six point-distinctness facts and three sigma^-1 values.

TWO DECISIONS THAT MADE IT TRACTABLE.

- **Successor form, not numerals.** Stating it as length `succ^4(q)` rather than `p >= 3` with
  indices 0,1,2 makes every index fact structural: `ZERO != succ(x)` from `succNotZero`,
  bounds from `lessThanIntro` with the gap written out. The alternative needs numeral
  arithmetic, and `arithmetic(peano)` CANNOT supply it: it treats `succ` opaquely, so every
  index fact with a VARIABLE under succ comes back "the statement is false" while the ground
  ones pass. Isolated that one conjunct at a time rather than guessing from the bulk failure.

- **Convert forward values, do not invert.** The arms want sigma^INVERSE values (the identities
  came from the commutator form at sigma^-1) and std's cycle tools give FORWARD values. Rather
  than build inversion machinery, `cycle.forwardGivesInverse` converts — one step from
  `inverseUndoesPermutation` plus a rewrite. The three conversions then line up exactly:
  sigma(s0)=s1 gives si(s1)=s0, and so on, which IS arm 1's si(j)=i.

TWO SMALL LEMMAS THE BRANCH NEEDED, both now in std:
  `cycle.forwardGivesInverse`             forward value -> inverse value
  `peano_order.natDisequalityIsSymmetric` a != b -> b != a over Nat, because `tautology` sees
                                          the two orientations as unrelated atoms

WHAT REMAINS for the covering theorem: the length-3 and length-2 branches, and the top-level
peel that splits on the peeled cycle's length. The length-2 branch is the one with content — it
needs parity to rule out a lone transposition, since an even permutation cannot be one. The
length-3 branch needs a SECOND peel (sigma is a 3-cycle, or a 3-cycle times a disjoint
remainder). Those are the larger half and are not probed.

